<header><a href="/">MAE Academy</a><nav aria-label="Primary"><a href="/simulations">Simulations</a> <a href="/research">Research</a> <a href="/library">Library</a> <a href="/about">About</a> <a href="/contact">Contact</a></nav></header>
<main>
<h1>Build, test, and read the system response.</h1><p>Applied laboratories for control, electronics, computation, mathematics, and connected engineering systems.</p>
<ul><li><a href="/simulations/control-systems">Control Systems</a> — Tune feedback, stability, and transient response inside a visible control loop.</li><li><a href="/simulations/circuit-lab">Electrical Circuits</a> — Probe current, voltage, and signal behavior across an interactive circuit bench.</li><li><a href="/simulations/power-electronics">Power Electronics</a> — Test switching topologies, conversion stages, and power-flow decisions.</li><li><a href="/simulations/code">Code Studio</a> — Interactive Multi-Language Simulation &amp; Trace Studio (MASM 8086, Python, C++, C).</li><li><a href="/simulations/plc">PLC</a> — Build ladder logic and observe industrial control sequences in motion.</li><li><a href="/simulations/signals-and-systems">Signals and Systems</a> — Transform, filter, and interpret signals across time and frequency domains.</li><li><a href="/simulations/digital-logic-design">Digital Logic Design</a> — Compose gates, truth states, and logic paths into working digital systems.</li><li><a href="/simulators/arduino-studio">Arduino Studio</a> — Embedded Systems &amp; Microcontrollers: write Arduino C++ and watch an interactive UNO board, serial monitor, and live plotter respond.</li><li><a href="/simulations/pneumatics-and-hydraulics">Pneumatics and Hydraulic Drive</a> — Interactive electro-pneumatic and hydraulic circuit simulation with animated component dissection.</li></ul>
<section><h2>How the MAE Academy simulators work</h2><p>MAE Academy is a free set of browser-based engineering labs. Each simulator runs a real numerical model behind the interface, so changing a parameter changes the computed response instead of replaying a canned animation. Nothing needs to be installed and there is no sign-up.</p><p>The labs are built for students and self-taught engineers who want to check a hand calculation, see why a circuit or controller behaves the way it does, or practise a technique before an exam or a project.</p>
<h3>What you can explore</h3><ul><li><a href="/simulations/plc">PLC and Siemens LOGO!</a><p>Write ladder logic or function block diagrams, force inputs and watch scan-by-scan outputs, timers and counters. Useful for learning interlocks, start/stop circuits and sequencing.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/arduino-studio">Arduino UNO</a><p>Run sketches against a virtual board with pins, a serial monitor and a plotter, so you can debug timing and I/O logic without hardware.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/code/python">Python, C, C++ and 8086 assembly</a><p>Step through programs line by line and see variables, memory, registers and the stack change, which makes pointers, recursion and flags much easier to understand.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/digital-logic-design?tool=schematic">Digital logic design</a><p>Build gate-level circuits, minimise expressions with Karnaugh maps, analyse timing diagrams and design finite state machines.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/signals-and-systems/fourier-analysis">Signals and systems</a><p>Explore convolution, Fourier series and transforms, Laplace and Z-transforms, and classify systems as linear, causal, stable or time-invariant.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/control-systems/root-locus">Control systems</a><p>Plot root locus, Bode and Nyquist diagrams, tune PID controllers against a step response and study state-space models.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/power-electronics/rectifier">Power electronics</a><p>Study controlled rectifiers, AC voltage controllers, DC-DC converters and PWM inverters with waveforms for voltage, current and harmonic content.</p></li><li><a href="/simulations/circuit-lab">Electrical circuits</a><p>Draw a schematic and run DC, AC and transient analysis to read node voltages and branch currents.</p></li></ul>
<h3>Getting the most out of a lab</h3><ul><li>Start from a known case, such as a textbook circuit or a transfer function from your course, and compare the result with your hand solution before changing anything.</li><li>Change one parameter at a time and note what moves. Most simulators show the governing equations or documentation below the workspace so you can connect the plot to the theory.</li><li>Treat results as a learning aid. For safety-critical or production designs, confirm with the relevant standards and your own verification.</li></ul></section>
